{% block content %}
  <main>
    <h1 class="section-title">Weather-Adjusted Pace</h1>
    <p class="lead">
      At 30°C with high humidity, the same pace costs you far more than on a cool morning. Enter
      your pace and your city, and see <strong>how much slower to run</strong> based on the
      forecast, and which is the <strong>coolest hour</strong> over the next 7 days.
    </p>

    <div class="calc wp">
      <div class="field">
        <label>Your pace in good weather</label>
        <div class="time-inputs wp-pace-inputs">
          <div><input type="number" id="wpMin" min="2" max="20" step="1" placeholder="5" aria-label="Minutes per kilometre" inputmode="numeric"><small>Minutes</small></div>
          <div><input type="number" id="wpSec" min="0" max="59" step="1" placeholder="20" aria-label="Seconds per kilometre" inputmode="numeric"><small>Seconds / km</small></div>
        </div>
      </div>

      <div class="field">
        <label for="wpQuery">Where will you run?</label>
        <div id="wpSearchBox">
          <div class="wp-search">
            <input type="search" id="wpQuery" placeholder="e.g. Thessaloniki" autocomplete="off" enterkeyhint="search">
            <button class="btn" id="wpSearchBtn" type="button">Search</button>
          </div>
          <button class="wp-link" id="wpGeoBtn" type="button">
            <svg viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" aria-hidden="true"><path d="M20 10c0 6-8 12-8 12s-8-6-8-12a8 8 0 0 1 16 0Z"/><circle cx="12" cy="10" r="3"/></svg>
            Use my location
          </button>
          <div class="wp-places" id="wpPlaces"></div>
        </div>
        <div class="wp-placebox calc-hidden" id="wpPlaceBox">
          <svg viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" aria-hidden="true"><path d="M20 10c0 6-8 12-8 12s-8-6-8-12a8 8 0 0 1 16 0Z"/><circle cx="12" cy="10" r="3"/></svg>
          <strong id="wpPlaceName"></strong>
          <button class="wp-link" id="wpChange" type="button">Change</button>
        </div>
      </div>

      <p class="wp-status" id="wpStatus" role="status" aria-live="polite"></p>

      <div class="calc-result calc-hidden" id="wpOut">
        <h2 class="wp-h">Pick a day and time</h2>
        <div class="wp-grid" id="wpGrid"></div>
        <ul class="wp-legend">
          <li><span class="wp-swatch lv-good"></span>Ideal</li>
          <li><span class="wp-swatch lv-fair"></span>Slightly warm</li>
          <li><span class="wp-swatch lv-hard"></span>Warm</li>
          <li><span class="wp-swatch lv-bad"></span>Very hot</li>
          <li><span class="wp-swatch lv-extreme"></span>Dangerous</li>
          <li><span class="wp-swatch wp-best"></span>Coolest hour</li>
        </ul>

        <div class="wp-result" aria-live="polite">
          <p class="wp-when" id="wpWhen"></p>
          <span class="wp-level" id="wpLevel"></span>
          <div class="pace">
            <div class="big" id="wpPace"></div>
            <span class="label" id="wpPaceLabel"></span>
          </div>
          <p class="wp-delta" id="wpDelta"></p>
          <table><tbody id="wpConditions"></tbody></table>
          <ul class="wp-tips" id="wpTips"></ul>
        </div>

        <p class="image-credit" style="margin-top:1rem;">
          * An estimate, not medical advice. Everyone handles heat differently, and your body adapts
          within 10–14 days of training in the heat. If you feel dizzy or nauseous, or you stop
          sweating, stop at once, find shade and drink water.
        </p>
        <p class="image-credit">Weather data: <a href="https://open-meteo.com/" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Open-Meteo.com</a> (CC BY 4.0)</p>
      </div>
    </div>

    <section class="wp-about">
      <h2 class="wp-h">How it works</h2>
      <p>
        Heat alone does not tell the whole story. What cools your body while you run is sweat
        evaporating, and the more moisture there is in the air, the harder that gets. That is why
        this tool looks at <strong>temperature</strong> and <strong>dew point</strong> together; the
        dew point shows how much water the air actually holds.
      </p>
      <p>
        It uses a practical rule many coaches apply: add the temperature and the dew point in degrees
        Fahrenheit. Up to 100, nothing changes. Above that, your pace slows gradually, up to about 10%
        when the sum reaches 180. Beyond that, hard running is not recommended. It is a rule from
        practice rather than an exact formula, but it agrees with studies showing that marathon times
        get steadily worse as temperature rises, and more so for slower runners.
      </p>
      <p>
        To learn more about what happens to your body in the heat, read the article on
        <a href="post-cardiac-drift-en.html">cardiac drift</a>.
      </p>
    </section>

    <div class="sources">
      <h4><svg viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" aria-hidden="true"><path d="M2 3h6a4 4 0 0 1 4 4v14a3 3 0 0 0-3-3H2z"/><path d="M22 3h-6a4 4 0 0 0-4 4v14a3 3 0 0 1 3-3h7z"/></svg>Sources</h4>
      <p>The science behind this tool:</p>
      <ol>
        <li>Ely, M. R., Cheuvront, S. N., Roberts, W. O., &amp; Montain, S. J. (2007). <cite>Impact of weather on marathon-running performance.</cite> Medicine &amp; Science in Sports &amp; Exercise, 39(3), 487–493. <a href="https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/?term=Impact+of+weather+on+marathon-running+performance+Ely" target="_blank" rel="noopener">PubMed →</a></li>
        <li>Ely, M. R., Martin, D. E., Cheuvront, S. N., &amp; Montain, S. J. (2008). <cite>Effect of ambient temperature on marathon pacing is dependent on runner ability.</cite> Medicine &amp; Science in Sports &amp; Exercise, 40(9), 1675–1680. <a href="https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/?term=Effect+of+ambient+temperature+on+marathon+pacing+is+dependent+on+runner+ability" target="_blank" rel="noopener">PubMed →</a></li>
        <li>Racinais, S., et al. (2015). <cite>Consensus recommendations on training and competing in the heat.</cite> British Journal of Sports Medicine, 49(18), 1164–1173. <a href="https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/?term=Consensus+recommendations+on+training+and+competing+in+the+heat" target="_blank" rel="noopener">PubMed →</a></li>
      </ol>
    </div>
  </main>
